Subject: [S] -- stamping name of function on a graph (summary)
Message-ID: <98Apr7.111009edt.17026@gateway.viropharma.com>

My original question was:

Suppose I have a function mygraph() that plots some data. I would like
to identify the plot with the date and time (easy, using stamp()), and
the name of the function that created the plot. My question is, how do I
capture the name of a function from within the function while it is
running? This way I can use stamp(get.func.name()). Please respond
directly to me.

Thanks to :

Bill Venables [wvenable at attunga.stats.adelaide.edu.au]
Alan Zaslavsky [zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu]
gary at sabot.com <mailto:gary at sabot.com> 
Bill Dunlap [bill at statsci.com]
Chuck Taylor [chuck at statsci.com]
ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk <mailto:ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk> 
Brian Dawkins [bdawkins at MCS.VUW.AC.NZ]
Patrick Burns [pburns at pburns.seanet.com]
Jerome Asselin [jasselin at mat.ulaval.ca]

Most offered suggestions about sys.call() and sys.parent(). The two I
found easiest are:

From:   Alan Zaslavsky [zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu]

I think the function whoffle() below will do the job:
get.func.name_function() as.character(sys.call(sys.parent(1))[[1]])

Examples: 
        > whoffle_function() get.func.name()
        > whoffle()
whoffle
        > whuffle_whoffle
        > whuffle()
whuffle
From:   Patrick Burns [pburns at pburns.seanet.com]

Here is a little example of how to do what I think you want.
        > fjj.par
function()
fjj.sub()
        > fjj.sub
function()
{
        c(this.fun = deparse(sys.call()[[1]]),
        parent.fun = deparse(sys.calls()[[ sys.parent()]][[1]]))
}

And here is the result:
        > fjj.par()
this.fun parent.fun
"fjj.sub" "fjj.par"
