From hodgess at uhdux2.dt.uh.edu  Tue Apr 21 22:16:57 1998
From: hodgess at uhdux2.dt.uh.edu (Erin Hodgess)
Date: Tue, 21 Apr 1998 17:16:57 -0500
Subject: [S] Matrix Square Root
Message-ID: <9804212216.AA04278@uhdux2.dt.uh.edu>

Dear Fei:

There's not a direct function to take the square root of a matrix,
but this is what you do:

If your matrix has real  non-neg. eigenvalues, real eigenvectors, is
invertible, then the sqrt(X) =
Eigvectors %*% Diag(sqrt(Eigenvalues)) %*% (Eigenvectors)^(-1).

Here is a little function to get you started:
 
sqrt1 <- function(x) {
        xe <- eigen(x)
        xe1 <- xe$values
        if(all(xe1 >= 0)) {
            xev1 <- diag(sqrt(xe1))
        }
        xval1 <- cbind(xe$vectors)
        xval1i <- solve(xval1)
        y <- xval1 %*% xev1 %*% xval1i
}

Good luck!

Sincerely,
Erin Hodgess
Assistant Professor
Dept. of Computer and Mathematical Sciences
University of Houston -- Downtown
